{"id":4699,"date":"2026-09-21T01:38:25","date_gmt":"2026-09-21T01:38:25","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/theshampoofactory.com\/?p=4699"},"modified":"2026-09-21T01:38:25","modified_gmt":"2026-09-21T01:38:25","slug":"oem-buying-faqs-moq-lead-time-certifications","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/theshampoofactory.com\/oem-buying-faqs-moq-lead-time-certifications\/","title":{"rendered":"OEM Buying FAQs: MOQ, Lead Time, Certifications"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>New to sourcing from a factory? This <strong>OEM buying FAQs<\/strong> page answers the questions every first-time shampoo or mask buyer asks \u2014 MOQ, lead time, certifications, pricing, IP, and the gotchas that surprise people. Bookmark it before you brief a partner, because the answers shape both your budget and your launch timeline.<\/p>\n<h2>MOQ (Minimum Order Quantity)<\/h2>\n<p><strong>What is a typical MOQ for shampoo or mask OEM?<\/strong> Often 500\u20131,000 units on stock packaging; custom tooling raises it. Stock jars and bottles let a factory amortize setup across many clients, which is why they accept lower minimums. Our low MOQ vs bulk guide shows how order size moves unit cost.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Can I start below 500 units?<\/strong> Some factories accept smaller pilots, but per-unit cost rises sharply and many plants simply will not run a line for less. Five hundred is a common floor for viable pricing; below it, you are often paying prototype rates.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Does MOQ differ by format?<\/strong> Yes. Oils and serums in glass often carry higher minimums because of filling-line setup; masks in heavy jars sit in the 1,000\u20133,000 range on stock components. Shampoo in standard bottles is usually the most flexible.<\/p>\n<h2>Lead Time<\/h2>\n<p><strong>How long from brief to delivery?<\/strong> Sampling 2\u20134 weeks; pilot production 2\u20134 weeks; bulk 4\u20138 weeks depending on actives and packaging. Our Guangdong launch story shows a real 2\u20133 month arc from first brief to sellable stock.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What delays lead time most?<\/strong> Custom packaging tooling and actives that need sourcing or maturation \u2014 oils especially (see our China hair oil OEM guide). Fragrance development and stability re-runs also push dates when they start late.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Can I compress the timeline?<\/strong> Partly. Using stock packaging, locking fragrance early, and testing stability on a scaled batch all save weeks. Custom tooling and late spec changes are the two things that reliably blow the schedule.<\/p>\n<h2>Certifications<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Which certs matter?<\/strong> GMP and ISO 22716 are baseline for cosmetics manufacturing. Market-specific rules then apply: the EU needs 1223\/2009 compliance with a responsible person; the US needs MoCRA registration. Vegan and COSMOS help premium and DACH channels (our Kaviarprotein guide covers the German angle).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Does the factory supply certs per shipment?<\/strong> A reliable partner does \u2014 insist on documentation at signing and per batch, not just once. A single certificate from a year ago proves nothing about the lot on today&#8217;s truck.<\/p>\n<p>Do not treat certification as a one-time checkbox. Retail buyers re-audit, and a lapsed COSMOS or vegan certificate can pull a product from a shelf mid-season. Build renewal dates into your calendar and confirm the factory&#8217;s own certificates remain current, since their lapse can invalidate yours.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What import docs do I need?<\/strong> Certificates of analysis, MSDS, ingredient origin letters, and market-specific notifications. Build these into the brief so they are not a surprise at customs.<\/p>\n<h2>Pricing &#038; IP<\/h2>\n<p><strong>How is pricing quoted?<\/strong> Usually per-unit ex-works, excluding freight and duty. Always ask for a full landed-cost view, because the ex-works number hides the ocean freight, tariff, and last-mile costs that decide whether the SKU is actually profitable.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Who owns my formula?<\/strong> With OEM, you should \u2014 get it in writing. ODM bases are the factory&#8217;s and may be sold to your competitor. Our OEM vs ODM guide explains the trade-off in detail, and the IP clause is the line you do not compromise on.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Can I protect against the factory reselling my product?<\/strong> Contractual exclusivity and a written formula-ownership clause are the tools. Exclusivity has a cost, but for a differentiated hero SKU it is usually worth it.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What payment terms are normal?<\/strong> A typical structure is 30% deposit to reserve the line and 70% against finished-goods inspection or before shipment. Avoid paying 100% upfront, and tie the final payment to a pre-shipment QC pass so you keep leverage if the batch fails spec.<\/p>\n<h2>Quality Control<\/h2>\n<p><strong>What QC should I require?<\/strong> Stability, microbial, viscosity, active assay, and fill-accuracy checks. Our plant-cost case study shows why in-line QC protects you from a bad batch reaching customers and triggering returns.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Should I visit the line?<\/strong> For a first serious order, yes \u2014 or use a third-party inspection at the pre-shipment stage. A 48-hour hold on the first pallet, as we describe in the caviar mask launch, catches line-specific defects before the whole run ships.<\/p>\n<h2>Common Mistakes First-Timers Make<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Accepting verbal IP or ownership assurances instead of getting them in writing.<\/li>\n<li>Ignoring landed cost and discovering the SKU is unprofitable only after freight and duty.<\/li>\n<li>Skipping per-batch certificates and getting burned at customs or on a complaint.<\/li>\n<li>Commissioning custom tooling before demand is proven, locking capital into a mould.<\/li>\n<li>Treating ex-works price as the real price and mis-budgeting the launch.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Each of these mistakes is recoverable individually but compounding in practice \u2014 a missed certificate and an unwritten IP clause together can sink a launch that was otherwise well formulated.<\/p>\n<h2>FAQ<\/h2>\n<p><strong>What is the minimum order quantity for OEM shampoo?<\/strong> Typically 500\u20131,000 units on stock packaging; custom tooling raises the floor. Oils and heavy jars often sit higher, while standard shampoo bottles are the most flexible format.<\/p>\n<p><strong>How long does OEM production take?<\/strong> Roughly 2\u20133 months from brief to pilot launch, longer for custom packaging or bulk runs that need tooling, maturation, or extra stability work.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Which certifications should my OEM partner have?<\/strong> GMP and ISO 22716 at minimum, plus market-specific compliance (EU 1223\/2009, US MoCRA) and any premium badges your channel needs, such as vegan or COSMOS.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Who owns the formula in an OEM agreement?<\/strong> You should, in writing \u2014 ODM formulas belong to the factory. The IP clause is the non-negotiable line, especially for a differentiated hero SKU.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What QC checks protect a first-time buyer?<\/strong> Stability, microbial, viscosity, active assay, and fill-accuracy, plus a pre-shipment inspection or a first-pallet hold to catch line-specific defects before the full run ships.<\/p>\n<h2>Conclusion &#038; Next Step<\/h2>\n<p>These <strong>OEM buying FAQs<\/strong> cover the fundamentals, but every brief is specific. The pattern across all of them is the same: get commitments in writing, price to landed cost, and require per-batch proof. Ready to turn questions into a real order? Explore Guangdong OEM capabilities on our sister site, and pair this with our anti-hair loss, hair mask cost, and gold caviar guides.<\/p>\n<p><!-- INTERLINK_NETWORK --><\/p>\n<h2>Explore our manufacturing network<\/h2>\n<p>Looking beyond this guide, our sister sites cover complementary angles:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/theshampoomanufacturer.com\/hair-care-routine-faqs-answered-by-a-formulation-team\/\">Hair Care Routine FAQs: Answered by a Formulation Team<\/a> \u2014 on our custom shampoo manufacturing site (theshampoomanufacturer.com)<\/li>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/guangzhoushampoo.com\/international-buyer-faqs-shipping-certs-samples\/\">International Buyer FAQs: Shipping, Certs, Samples<\/a> \u2014 on our Guangzhou shampoo factory site (guangzhoushampoo.com)<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><script type=\"application\/ld+json\">\n{\n  \"@context\": \"https:\/\/schema.org\",\n  \"@type\": \"FAQPage\",\n  \"mainEntity\": [\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \"name\": \"What is the minimum order quantity for OEM shampoo?\",\n      \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \"text\": \"Typically 500\u20131,000 units on stock packaging; custom tooling raises the floor. Oils and heavy jars often sit higher, while standard shampoo bottles are the most flexible format.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \"name\": \"How long does OEM production take?\",\n      \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \"text\": \"Roughly 2\u20133 months from brief to pilot launch, longer for custom packaging or bulk runs that need tooling, maturation, or extra stability work.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \"name\": \"Which certifications should my OEM partner have?\",\n      \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \"text\": \"GMP and ISO 22716 at minimum, plus market-specific compliance (EU 1223\/2009, US MoCRA) and any premium badges your channel needs, such as vegan or COSMOS.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \"name\": \"Who owns the formula in an OEM agreement?\",\n      \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \"text\": \"You should, in writing \u2014 ODM formulas belong to the factory. The IP clause is the non-negotiable line, especially for a differentiated hero SKU.\"\n      }\n    }